User: A standard deck of playing cards with $26$ red cards and $26$ black cards is split into two piles, each having at least one card. In pile $A$ there are six times as many black cards as red cards. In pile $B,$ the number of red cards is a multiple of the number of black cards. How many red cards are in pile $B?$ Write your answer in \boxed{} format.

Assistant: Fix a red card in $A$. Since the number of red cards in $A$ is equal to the number of black cards in $A,$ the number of red cards in $A$ is a multiple of the number of black cards in $A.$ In addition, since the number of red cards in $A$ is a multiple of the number of black cards in $A,$ the number of red cards in $A$ must be equal to the the number of black cards in $A$ or to twice the number of black cards in $A,$ or to $3$ times the number of black cards in $A.$ (If we were to match up all the remaining red cards with black cards, some red cards would remain, and if we match up more red cards with black cards, we would run out of red cards.)
Case 1 ($A$ is a red card): Suppose that $A$ is a red card.
Then,Notice that if we set up this pair of definitions
$$ \displaystyle \mbox{Red Cards in A} = \mbox{Red Cards in B} \times 26. \mbox{Black Cards in A}=\mbox{Black Cards in B}.$$
We can see that the first expression states that the number of red cards in A equals the number of black cards in A, and the second expression combined with the first one states that the number of red cards in A equal the number of black cards in $A\times26.$ Therefore, there are 26 black cards in $A$ or there are 26 black cards in $A$ or there are 52 black cards in $A.$ In all these cases, there are 26 black cards in $B$ (by the 2nd and 3rd expression). Also notice that in this case it goes to say that the number of black cards in pile B is 52 when 26 black cards in pile $A$ (otherwise all the black cards would be in pile $A$). This is not possible since all the black cards need to be a multiple of the number of red cards in pile $B$. In this situation this would mean the number of red cards need to be 26.  So in the case $A$ is a red card, we can say that there are 13 red cards in A, 13 black cards in A, and 26 black cards in $B.$
Case 2 ($A$ is a black card): Suppose that $A$ is a black card.
Then, notice that if we set up this pair of definitions
$$ \displaystyle \mbox{Black Cards in A}=\mbox{Black Cards in B} \times 13.$$
We can see that the first expression states that the number of black cards in A equals the number of red cards in B and the second expression states that the number of red cards in pile $B$ is a multiple of the number of black cards in pile $B.$ We know, however, that the number of red cards in pile $B$ should be greater than the number of red cards in pile $A$ or at least half the number of red cards in pile $A$. So we can divide the number of red cards in the $B$ into 2. We know it is 2 (if not, it would mean that the and number of black cards is 13) and thus is true the number of red cards is the same.
In this case, however, it would immediately follow that \boxed{13} is exactly the number of red cards red cards.<|end_of_text|>
