User: Let $P$ be the product of the first $100$ positive odd integers. Find the largest integer $k$ such that $P$ is divisible by $3^k.$
 Write your answer in \boxed{} format.

Assistant: We have that $P$ is the product of the first 100 positive odd integers, which is the product of 100 integers that are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9,... and so on. Thus
\[P = (2)(2)(2) \cdot \cdots \cdot 100 \cdot (100)(99) \cdots 1.\]
If we absorb the $2$'s, neglect the $1$'s, and multiply the resulting terms together, we have
\begin{align*}
P &= (2)(2)(2) \cdot \cdots \cdot (1) \cdot (100)(101) \cdot (99)(97) \cdots (5)(3)\\
&= (2)(2)(2) \cdot \cdots \cdot (1)(100)(101) \cdot (99)(97) \cdots (5)(3)\\
&= (2)(2)(2) \cdot \cdots \cdot (1)(100)(101) \cdot (101)(102) \cdot \cdots \cdot (5)(3)\\
&= (2)(2)(2) \cdot \cdots \cdot (1)(100)(101)(102) \cdot \cdots \cdot (5)(6)
\end{align*}
Hence, \begin{align*}
P \equiv &(1)(0)\!
\pmod{3^2},\\
P \equiv &(2)(1)(2)\!
\pmod{3^3},\\
P \equiv &(1)\!
\pmod{3^3},\\
\end{align*} 
And we have that
 $P$ will be divisible by $3^k$ with $k \leq 2,$ and hence $k$ must be $2.$<|end_of_text|>
