\#\text{ ways to choose President: } &\,  \\ 
\#\text{ ways to choose Vice-presidents: } &\, 15 \ \text{C}_5\\
\#\text{ ways to choose Secretary: } &\, \left(\,#\text{ chances that the Secretary is not re-elected: }\right)\left(\, 15\ \text{C}_5 \right)+\left(\,1\ \text{C}_5\right) \\ 
\#\text{ ways to choose Treasurer: }&\,  \\
\end{align*} Substituting in the numbers (5 = S, 10 = P, 13 = V) \begin{align*}
\#\text{ ways to choose President: } &\,  4 \ \text{C}_1\\
\#\text{ ways to choose Vice-presidents: } &\, \left(\,  10 \ \text{C}_5\right)+\left(\, 1 \ \text{C}_5\right) = \boxed{432} \\
\#\text{ ways to choose Secretary or Treasurer: }&\,  \\
\#\text{ ways to choose Secretary: } &\, \left(132 \ \text{C}_2\right)+\left(4 \ \text{C}_2\right)+\left(113 \ \text{C}_4\right)+\left(13 \ \text{C}_4\right)\\
\#\text{ ways to choose Treasurer: }&\, \left(132 \ \text{C}_2\right)+\left(4 \ \text{C}_2\right)\left(\, 9 \ \text{C}_5 \right)  \ \text{Oops!}\ \\ &\,  1123 \text{C}_6 \\
\#\text{ ways to choose all offices: } &\,  \\
\end{align*} This problem requires a bit of addition and multiplication in order to get the answer. Notice the order in which the two ways are highlighted, first the people that can be chosen as secretary and then people that can be chosen as treasurer.<|end_of_text|>
