User: There are 5 blue chips and 3 yellow chips in a bag. One chip is drawn from the bag. That chip is placed back into the bag. A second chip is then drawn. What is the probability that the two selected chips are of different colors? Express your answer as a common fraction. Write your answer in \boxed{} format.

Assistant: Using the formula for probability, P(A and B) = P(A) P(B|A), you can find the probability P(B|A) which is the probability of drawing a yellow chip given a blue chip is drawn. That is \frac{3}{8}. The probability P(A), or that a blue chip is drawn, is \frac{5}{8}. Plugging in the values, you get \frac{3}{8}\times\frac{5}{8} = \frac{15}{64}.

Yet because you must draw another chip, you must also use the formula for conditional probability. If two chips are drawn that have been different, the last chip is drawn from the remaining chips which are not blue or yellow and are in just as great of a proportion, because there is no way of knowing whether the first chip drawn is blue or yellow. Thus, in order to identify the correct portion, the possible number of chips from which a blue chip can be chosen will be reduced by 1 since the first chip is blue and is not replaced back into the set. It is, in fact, also vertically obtained by taking \frac{6}{8} = 1 - \frac{1}{8}\times 5, which can be translated back to 1- \frac{5}{8}, which equals \frac{3}{8} to a major extent nearing our initial answer of \frac{5}{8}.<|end_of_text|>
